const august18EvidenceAddenda: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  'legal-support-email-triage-boundary-study': 'Email triage is especially vulnerable to hidden judgment because routing labels can influence who sees a message first. The review sample should therefore compare the incoming message, the supplied rule, the assigned queue, and the reason for any exception. A forwarded chain should retain its original timestamps and recipients; an attachment should remain linked to the message that supplied it. If the worker sees a request for advice, an allegation, or a possible deadline, the record should quote the request neutrally and stop. The reviewer can then decide whether the item belongs in a legal queue, requires a client response, or needs a different owner. This test measures whether the administrative route is reproducible, not whether the worker correctly predicted legal importance.',
  'law-firm-document-metadata-quality-research': 'Metadata cleanup should be tested as a provenance problem. A filename, matter number, author field, or date can be mechanically compared, but a missing value may reflect a source gap rather than a mistake to be filled from context. The sample should include an accurate record, a record with two plausible matter numbers, a scanned file with no reliable creation date, and a superseded version. Support records the observed value, the source used, the proposed administrative correction, and the field left unresolved. The owner decides whether the correction is safe and whether the document belongs in the matter. The evidence supports better retrieval and reviewability; it does not prove that a corrected metadata field establishes ownership, authorship, or legal effect.',
  'legal-support-client-portal-permission-review': 'Permission review should compare intended task, actual capability, and approval evidence as three separate facts. A read-only role may still expose an overly broad matter set, while an upload permission may create an external-sharing risk if the recipient is wrong. A useful sample includes a correctly scoped user, a former participant whose access should be removed, a guest with an unclear relationship, and a permission inherited from a group. Support records the portal state and the request as supplied, then routes discrepancies. The firm owner decides entitlement, consent, and remediation. This makes the review useful across an offshore support arrangement without treating a portal screenshot as proof that every access decision was authorized.',
  'legal-intake-duplicate-party-review': 'Duplicate-party review requires deliberate resistance to false certainty. Similar names, addresses, email domains, and registration identifiers can be useful comparison fields, but each field has a different error pattern. The sample should preserve the values from each intake record and show which comparison rule produced the flag. It should include a common name shared by unrelated people, a corporate name with a spelling variation, and a record with a missing identifier. Support may flag, link, and request clarification. It should not merge records, label a person as the same party, or decide whether a conflict exists. The reviewer’s disposition should remain attached to the evidence so later changes do not erase why the flag was raised.',
  'legal-support-deadline-source-reconciliation': 'Date reconciliation is strongest when it treats disagreement as an output. The sample should include a date in a signed document, a date in a task system, a date supplied in an email, and a date whose time zone is unstated. Support records the source, field label, time zone if supplied, and the exact difference. It may calculate a neutral interval or sort the records chronologically, but it should not call one date controlling because it appears most recent. The owner decides whether a deadline exists, what event starts it, and what action is required. This distinction lets legal support make conflicting records visible while avoiding the dangerous appearance that a populated calendar has resolved legal effect.',
  'legal-support-redaction-candidate-evidence': 'Candidate evidence must preserve both the original context and the reason a location was flagged. A mechanical rule may identify a personal identifier, a supplied term, or a page region for review, but the same text can have different significance depending on the matter and production instruction. The sample should include a true candidate, a false positive, a repeated phrase, and a location where the surrounding text changes interpretation. Support records page, field, source version, rule, and uncertainty, and leaves the original unchanged. Counsel decides privilege, responsiveness, confidentiality, waiver, and final redaction. A candidate index is valuable because it exposes review work; it becomes unsafe when its labels are presented as final legal decisions.',
  'law-firm-knowledge-base-source-expiry': 'Source expiry is not simply a calendar reminder. An instruction may become unsafe because a cited rule changed, a system field was renamed, a client restriction was added, or the original owner left. The review sample should compare the knowledge entry with its source, owner, approval date, review date, and current task examples. Support can flag a broken link, conflicting version, missing owner, or overdue review and quarantine the entry under an approved state. The owner decides whether to revise, retire, or reapprove it. This evidence distinguishes a stale internal instruction from a legal conclusion and prevents a support worker from silently turning an old example into a current rule.',
  'legal-support-matter-status-evidence': 'Status evidence should be tested against the temptation to convert activity into progress. A document upload proves that a file was uploaded; it does not prove that counsel reviewed it. A task marked complete proves a field changed; it does not prove that the requested work was sufficient. The sample should include a completed action with no supporting file, a stale owner field, conflicting systems, and a pending client request. Support records each observable event and labels the unknown. The firm owner decides whether the matter is on track, whether a client update is appropriate, and whether further work is needed. That discipline makes a status report useful without smuggling predictions into administrative language.',
  'legal-support-translation-source-preservation': 'Translation preservation should be examined at the level of meaning-bearing features, not only file count. The sample should include a table, a signature block, a defined term, a handwritten mark, an attachment, and an unreadable scan. Support records the source page, working version, omitted or uncertain feature, supplied glossary, and reviewer question. It can compare layout and terminology against explicit instructions, but it cannot decide whether a phrase carries a different legal effect or whether the output is fit for filing. The reviewer determines semantic accuracy, certification, delivery, and client communication. Keeping the source and working versions together gives the firm an inspectable handoff even when the administrative comparison cannot answer the linguistic question.',
  'legal-support-incident-timeline-reconstruction': 'Timeline reconstruction should preserve disagreement between systems instead of smoothing it away. The sample should include a failed login, a successful login, a permission change, a download, a clock difference, duplicate records, and a missing segment. Support records each event identifier, source system, retrieval query, timestamp and time zone as shown, and any gap. It may sort the records for review, but it should not merge accounts, infer intent, or describe a disclosure. The incident owner decides which events are related, what evidence must be preserved, and whether legal or client-facing action follows. The value of the support lane is a protected factual packet that helps authorized reviewers reason without turning a chronology into an accusation.'
};
const august18LegacyRepairedPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [
  {slug:'legal-support-email-triage-boundary-study',title:'Legal support email triage: research on routing without legal judgment',excerpt:'An evidence study of how supervised legal support can preserve email context while leaving urgency and advice decisions with the firm.',published:'2026-08-18',sourceDate:'2026-08-18',cluster:'Scope Benchmarks',headlineStat:'A source-linked study of message context, routing evidence, and escalation.',relatedSlugs:['legal-intake-support-control-benchmark','legal-document-version-control'],thumbnail:'/research-thumbnails/research-default.svg',sections:[
    {heading:'Research question',body:'On August 18, 2026, this study asks a narrow question for LegalServicesOffshore.com: can a supervised legal support worker route an incoming law-firm email while preserving enough context for an attorney to decide significance? Email is not a neutral queue. One thread may contain a client request, a forwarded court notice, an attachment, and several unrelated replies. The research question therefore concerns the handoff, not faster inbox clearing. The worker may identify the supplied matter label, sender, recipients, timestamp, attachment, and approved routing rule. The worker must not infer that a message is privileged, urgent, responsive, or suitable for a client reply. The relevant output is a traceable observation with an exception when the supplied facts do not fit the rule.'},
    {heading:'Methodology and evidence scope',body:'The method compares ABA Formal Opinion 477R on protecting client information, NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 on identifying and managing risk, and the OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet on attributable event records. These sources address professional responsibility, security governance, and application logging; none is a universal inbox procedure. I treated each source as evidence for a control principle, then applied those principles to four hypothetical law-firm messages: a routine document, a possible deadline notice, a forwarded multi-matter chain, and an unknown attachment. I compared what a support role can observe with what requires an authorized legal or client-facing decision. This is qualitative process research, not a survey, benchmark, compliance certificate, or claim about performance.'},
    {heading:'Finding: context is part of the record',body:'The comparison supports preserving the message as received rather than reducing it to a queue label. A useful triage record links the original message, exact received time, sender and recipients, subject, attachment names, supplied matter identifier, rule applied, and routing destination. If a thread is forwarded, its earlier timestamps and participants remain relevant context. If a file arrives without a reliable matter label, the absence is itself evidence. A support worker can record “matter not confirmed” and route the item to the named owner. Replacing that fact with a guessed folder creates a false appearance of certainty. For offshore legal support, context preservation is practical risk control because the firm-side reviewer can reconstruct the path without asking the worker to remember a private conversation.'},
    {heading:'Finding: routing is not legal assessment',body:'A queue assignment can look like a conclusion even when it was intended as administration. “Urgent,” “privileged,” “client,” and “filing” are not interchangeable labels. A support role may apply a firm-approved mechanical rule, such as routing a message from a named mailbox to a specified review queue, but should record the rule and its limits. A message that mentions a court, threat, advice request, or deadline should preserve the words and escalate rather than paraphrase them into a promise. The attorney decides what event matters, what response is required, and whether a client communication is authorized. The evidence supports a boundary in which the worker prepares a factual packet while counsel retains judgment.'},
    {heading:'Operational test',body:'A firm can test this boundary using a small, stated cohort of representative messages. Include a complete message with a confirmed matter, a notice whose date type is unclear, a chain containing two matters, and an attachment from an unknown sender. For each item, compare the original, the fields recorded, the rule cited, the assigned queue, and the exception state. Classify observations as missing context, wrong matter, unsupported urgency, altered source, or missed escalation. Retain the denominator and review period. A reviewer should inspect the first cohort and revise the task definition when the same ambiguity recurs. This test measures reconstructability of routing, not legal accuracy, response time, or the chance that a message produces a particular outcome.'},
    {heading:'Role boundary and limitations',body:'The worker may preserve messages, enter supplied facts, attach source links, compare explicit routing fields, and prepare a handoff. The worker may not decide privilege, deadline effect, conflict, responsiveness, strategy, advice, filing sufficiency, or client entitlement. The cited sources do not determine every jurisdiction, engagement term, retention policy, or portal setting. They do not prove that offshore support is safe or unsafe, and a complete log can still contain an inaccurate source. Email systems may omit metadata, clocks may differ, and a sender identity may be spoofed. The firm must set local instructions, permissions, retention, sampling, and escalation contacts. A matter-specific legal question remains with the firm’s authorized professional.'},
    {heading:'Evidence-led conclusion',body:'Evidence-led conclusion: email triage can be a defensible supervised support lane when the service is defined as source-preserving routing rather than legal assessment. The strongest design records the original context, the mechanical rule, the uncertainty, and the reviewer who accepts the handoff. A queue is not a finding, and a completed triage mark is not proof that a deadline, privilege issue, or client duty was resolved. For LegalServicesOffshore.com, the useful research standard is whether an attorney can see what arrived, what the worker did, what the worker deliberately did not decide, and why the item was escalated. If those facts are not recoverable, the scope is too broad or the evidence design is incomplete.'},{heading:'Case-specific implication',body:'The email case exposes a failure mode that a generic document queue can hide: sequence changes meaning. A reply received after a client instruction may contain a correction, while a forwarded notice may preserve an earlier time that the newest message does not repeat. The support record should therefore retain ordering and relationship, not just the latest text. A message with no attachment can still require review if it changes an instruction. Conversely, an attachment name alone does not establish that the file belongs to the matter. These observations support a deliberate pause state. The firm can later decide whether to merge, respond, calendar, or investigate, but the initial administrative record must not pre-answer those questions.'}],sources:[researchSources[0],researchSources[1],researchSources[10],researchSources[2]]},
  {slug:'law-firm-document-metadata-quality-research',title:'Law-firm document metadata quality: research on provenance before cleanup',excerpt:'A research study of metadata correction, source fidelity, and attorney review in supervised document support.',published:'2026-08-18',sourceDate:'2026-08-18',cluster:'Evidence Quality',headlineStat:'A source-linked study of document identity, version history, and unresolved fields.',relatedSlugs:['law-firm-document-intake-indexing','legal-document-version-control'],thumbnail:'/research-thumbnails/research-default.svg',sections:[
    {heading:'Research question',body:'This August 18, 2026 report asks whether a supervised legal support worker can improve document metadata quality without turning a missing field into an invented fact. In a law-firm matter, a filename, author, date, or matter number can help retrieval, yet the same field may carry implications about ownership, authorship, or legal significance. The research question is what may be observed and compared from supplied sources, and what must be reserved for the matter owner. The worker can preserve the original, transcribe visible values, compare them with an approved record, and flag a discrepancy. The worker should not select between two plausible matter numbers, infer the author of a scan, or overwrite the source to make the database appear complete.'},
    {heading:'Methodology and evidence scope',body:'I reviewed ABA Formal Opinion 477R, NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, and NIST Zero Trust Architecture, then examined the metadata handoff as a qualitative control problem. The evidence scope covers confidentiality, least-privilege access, attributable changes, and provenance. Four hypothetical items test different conditions: a clean native file, a scanned exhibit with no reliable creation date, two files with plausible matter identifiers, and a superseded version that resembles the current copy. The study separates what the sources say about protection and traceability from the operating inference for offshore legal support. It is not a data-quality benchmark and does not establish a universal filename policy, retention rule, authorship finding, or legal outcome.'},
    {heading:'Finding: an empty field is evidence',body:'Metadata work becomes unsafe when absence is treated as an invitation to guess. A blank author field may mean the source was scanned, exported, or stripped; a date may be a filesystem timestamp rather than the date shown in the document; a matter number may have been copied from an email rather than verified in the firm system. The support record should distinguish observed value, source location, comparison value, and unresolved status. A proposed correction can be prepared as a suggestion without changing the original. This distinction helps an attorney review the basis of the proposed edit. It also prevents an offshore support role from manufacturing provenance while trying to satisfy a completeness metric.'},
    {heading:'Finding: version history matters more than polish',body:'The evidence favors an append-only account of changes. If a file is renamed, the former name, source path, checksum or other approved identifier, actor, time, and reason supplied by the firm should remain available. If two versions differ, the worker can list the observable difference and link both copies. The worker cannot declare one operative, authentic, final, or superseded unless the firm’s explicit instruction authorizes that factual status. A clean metadata panel may make a document easier to find, but it cannot prove that the document belongs in a matter or that its date has legal effect. Quality therefore means reconstructable provenance, not merely populated fields.'},
    {heading:'Practical research test',body:'The proposed control can be tested with a fixed sample drawn from one defined matter type and period. Reviewers should compare source file, extracted field, proposed change, actual change, and disposition. Useful categories include wrong source, copied value without provenance, stale instruction, conflicting identifier, lost version, and unresolved field correctly escalated. The firm should inspect whether the worker preserved the original and whether a reviewer can repeat the comparison. If the same field repeatedly requires judgment, remove it from routine support or add a firm-owned decision gate. A small sample cannot prove accuracy across all document types; it reveals where the task definition is sufficiently explicit for supervised preparation.'},
    {heading:'Boundaries and limitations',body:'The worker may catalog, link, transcribe, compare, preserve, and flag. The worker may not certify authenticity, decide authorship, determine ownership, merge records, alter substantive content, or treat metadata as a legal conclusion. ABA, NIST, and zero-trust guidance do not answer every court rule, client restriction, discovery protocol, or records policy. File systems may expose unreliable timestamps; conversion tools may change metadata; and access logs may not capture every local action. A firm must define source authority, approved systems, retention, permissions, and review. Cross-border handling and matter-specific questions may require jurisdiction-specific advice. This study offers a bounded design, not a compliance determination.'},
    {heading:'Case-specific implication',body:'Metadata quality is especially sensitive to conversion. A word-processing export can preserve visible text while changing creation information, embedded comments, or the relationship between an attachment and its parent file. A scanned exhibit may show a handwritten date that is not represented in machine fields. The support worker can record that the value is visible on page two, that the file field is blank, and that the source was not altered. That precision gives the reviewer two distinct questions: is the metadata entry accurate, and is the document itself the correct source? Treating those as one cleanup task encourages silent assumptions. The evidence favors separate observations, separate proposed edits, and a firm-owned disposition.'}],sources:[researchSources[0],researchSources[1],researchSources[2],researchSources[10]]},
  {slug:'legal-support-client-portal-permission-review',title:'Legal support client-portal permission review: research on access evidence',excerpt:'An evidence study of task scope, inherited permissions, and firm-owned decisions in client portals.',published:'2026-08-18',sourceDate:'2026-08-18',cluster:'Access Controls',headlineStat:'A source-linked study of intended access, observed capability, and approval evidence.',relatedSlugs:['legal-access-deprovisioning-workflow','legal-support-offshore-portal-upload-checklist'],thumbnail:'/research-thumbnails/research-default.svg',sections:[
    {heading:'Research question',body:'This August 18, 2026 study asks: what evidence lets a law firm review whether a support role’s client-portal access matches the task, without asking the worker to decide entitlement or consent? A portal permission is not a single fact. A user may have read access to too many matters, upload permission to the wrong workspace, or inherited capability through a group. The worker can record the requested task, visible role, matter scope, sharing setting, and approval reference. The worker cannot decide whether a person should see confidential material, whether a former participant remains authorized, or whether a client has consented. The research focuses on an inspectable access review, not a recommendation to grant broad access.'},
    {heading:'Methodology and evidence scope',body:'The method compares ABA Formal Opinion 477R, NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, NIST SP 800-207 on zero trust, and CISA cloud security architecture. Together they support examining purpose, identity, least privilege, verification, and traceable change, but they do not supply a law-firm authorization matrix. I applied those principles to four hypothetical observations: a correctly scoped read-only user, an inherited group permission, a former project participant, and a requested upload to an external recipient. The analysis distinguishes an observed portal state from the firm’s legal or contractual authorization. It is qualitative and bounded; it measures whether evidence supports review, not whether a specific system is secure or compliant.'},
    {heading:'Three facts must stay separate',body:'Permission review is clearest when intended task, actual capability, and approval evidence are recorded as separate fields. A request to upload an approved exhibit does not prove that upload permission is needed for every matter. A portal screenshot can show a role at one moment, but it may not show inherited scope, group membership, or the reason an account remains active. Support can compare the visible state with supplied criteria and identify a mismatch. It should preserve the screenshot or system record under approved controls, note when it was observed, and route the discrepancy. The owner decides entitlement, remediation, and client communication. Conflating these facts makes a technical observation look like an authorization decision.'},
    {heading:'Finding: least privilege is also role clarity',body:'The sources suggest that narrow access reduces exposure and makes exceptions easier to detect, but least privilege does not answer every professional question. A read-only account may still expose an unnecessarily broad matter set. An upload account may create a disclosure risk if the destination is wrong. A support worker therefore needs a task-specific permission brief: systems, records, permitted action, prohibited action, duration, reviewer, and stop condition. The worker can identify that a role appears broader than the brief. The worker should not self-correct by deleting access, moving a file, or notifying a client unless the firm has explicitly authorized that mechanical action. Remediation remains attached to the owner’s decision.'},
    {heading:'Review design',body:'A useful pilot samples active and inactive accounts across one portal and one defined period. Include an ordinary user, a group-inherited role, a guest with unclear relationship, and a user whose task ended. For each, preserve request, identity, visible capability, matter scope, approval record, observed discrepancy, and disposition. Categorize overbroad scope, stale account, missing approval, unexpected sharing, and unclear owner. The reviewer should test whether the support record explains what was seen without exposing more client data than necessary. Sampling provides local evidence about the review process; it does not prove that every account is correct or that a portal configuration prevents all disclosure.'},
    {heading:'Boundary and limitations',body:'The support role may inventory supplied access facts, compare them with explicit criteria, preserve evidence, and route exceptions. It may not decide consent, privilege, confidentiality, client entitlement, incident significance, or whether access should be revoked unless the firm has given a precise mechanical instruction. The cited sources do not determine every contract, jurisdiction, portal feature, identity-proofing rule, or retention obligation. Screenshots can be incomplete, group membership can change, and logs can be unavailable. The firm must own authorization, review cadence, emergency handling, and any notification. Offshore location does not move professional responsibility to the support worker or turn a technical check into legal advice.'},
    {heading:'Case-specific implication',body:'Portal review benefits from testing negative space: what the account can see but should not need, what the user can do but was not asked to do, and what the record cannot show about inherited access. A support worker should record the scope shown by the system and avoid exploring unrelated matters merely to satisfy curiosity. If a permission screen cannot reveal the effective recipient or group, that limitation belongs in the handoff. The owner can then choose a safer verification path. This is different from declaring the portal defective. The research supports an evidence packet that makes uncertainty actionable while keeping remediation, notification, and access entitlement under firm control.'}],sources:[researchSources[0],researchSources[1],researchSources[2],researchSources[9]]},
  {slug:'legal-intake-duplicate-party-review',title:'Legal intake duplicate-party review: research on flags before identity decisions',excerpt:'A research study of comparison evidence, false certainty, and controlled escalation in legal intake.',published:'2026-08-18',sourceDate:'2026-08-18',cluster:'Intake Evidence',headlineStat:'A source-linked study of duplicate signals without identity or conflict conclusions.',relatedSlugs:['law-firm-document-intake-indexing','legal-intake-source-verification'],thumbnail:'/research-thumbnails/research-default.svg',sections:[
    {heading:'Research question',body:'On August 18, 2026, this report asks whether a supervised legal intake role can flag possible duplicate parties while leaving identity, conflict, and representation decisions with the firm. Similar names are common, but similarity is not identity. A company abbreviation, shared address, email domain, registration number, or transliteration may support a comparison while still producing a false match. The worker can preserve the values supplied in each intake record, state which fields were compared, and route a discrepancy. The worker cannot merge records, label two people as the same, determine a conflict, or tell a prospective client that an intake issue is resolved. The research concerns evidence quality at the point before a professional decision.'},
    {heading:'Methodology and evidence scope',body:'I compared ABA Formal Opinion 477R, NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, and ICO data-protection-by-design guidance, then tested their recurring principles against four hypothetical intake pairs: unrelated people with a common name, a corporate spelling variation, a missing identifier, and two records with conflicting addresses. The unit of analysis is the supplied value, comparison rule, flag, and reviewer disposition. This is a qualitative design study, not an identity-resolution accuracy test, conflict check, or claim about a particular jurisdiction. The external sources inform confidentiality, minimization, purpose, and traceability. They do not decide what information a firm may use for a particular conflict or engagement process.'},
    {heading:'Signals are not findings',body:'A duplicate flag is best treated as a request for review. The record should show the exact fields compared and preserve the source values rather than collapsing them into a confidence score. If the name matches but the address is absent, that absence matters. If a company has several legal entities, the worker should record the supplied registration information and the unresolved relationship. A support worker can link records, quote the apparent discrepancy, and assign the approved review queue. The firm decides whether the records describe one party, related parties, or unrelated parties, and whether any professional rule follows. Keeping the flag neutral prevents a convenient intake label from becoming a hidden legal conclusion.'},
    {heading:'Why minimization changes the design',body:'Duplicate review can tempt a team to collect more identifiers than the intake purpose requires. The evidence supports a narrower approach: use only fields approved for the defined check, restrict access to the records needed, and preserve an explanation of why the comparison was made. A worker should not search unrelated matters, enrich a record from public sources without instruction, or copy sensitive information into an uncontrolled spreadsheet. If the supplied data is insufficient, the safe output is insufficiency plus escalation. This is useful offshore legal support because the worker’s value is disciplined preparation. The firm’s owner remains responsible for deciding what additional information is appropriate and what the flag means.'},
    {heading:'Study design for a firm',body:'A firm can evaluate the process with a known cohort of intake records and a stated review period. Include obvious nonmatches, likely duplicates, incomplete forms, and a pair where the same name belongs to different entities. Reviewers should compare source values, rule applied, flag reason, access used, and final disposition. Measure categories of correction rather than publishing a single match rate: wrong source, overcollection, unsupported merge, missed escalation, and correctly preserved uncertainty. The sample is local process evidence and should not be generalized to all names, languages, entities, or jurisdictions. When reviewers repeatedly override a rule, the rule needs redesign or removal from routine support.'},
    {heading:'Role boundary and limitations',body:'The role may transcribe, compare explicit fields, preserve intake sources, create a neutral flag, and route. It may not decide identity, conflict, eligibility, representation, materiality, consent, or client advice. ABA, NIST, and ICO guidance do not settle every local rule, engagement term, retention period, or firm-specific conflict procedure. Names can be transliterated, addresses can change, identifiers can be mistyped, and a public match can be misleading. A well-recorded flag is not proof of sameness, and a reviewer’s decision may change as new facts arrive. The firm must define permitted fields, approved systems, owner review, and deletion or retention rules for the intake evidence.'},
    {heading:'Evidence-led conclusion',body:'The research supports duplicate-party review as a bounded intake preparation task, not as automated identity judgment. The strongest record preserves the original values, names the comparison rule, records what is missing, and leaves the disposition with the authorized firm owner. For LegalServicesOffshore.com, the practical test is whether a reviewer can understand why a flag was raised without mistaking the flag for a conflict finding or representation decision. A disciplined offshore support lane can reduce clerical ambiguity while keeping the professional question visible. When the evidence is incomplete or contradictory, an explicit unresolved state is a higher-quality result than a merged record that merely makes the queue look finished.'},{heading:'Case-specific implication',body:'A duplicate flag should be explainable to a reviewer who did not create it. For an individual, that may mean preserving the exact spelling, supplied address, and missing identifier without copying unrelated personal data. For an entity, it may mean retaining the legal-name variation and registration value separately rather than normalizing both into one preferred label. The support worker can show which comparison produced the flag and which source was unavailable. That record is more useful than a confidence number because it identifies the question the firm must answer. If the reviewer later rejects the match, the original flag should remain visible as historical evidence, not be rewritten as if the ambiguity never existed.'}],sources:[researchSources[0],researchSources[1],researchSources[6],researchSources[5]]},
  ...august18ExplicitBasePosts.filter(p => ['legal-support-deadline-source-reconciliation','legal-support-redaction-candidate-evidence','law-firm-knowledge-base-source-expiry','legal-support-matter-status-evidence','legal-support-translation-source-preservation','legal-support-incident-timeline-reconstruction'].includes(p.slug)),
].map(p => ({...p, sections:[...p.sections,
  {heading:'Research methodology', body:`This direct August 18, 2026 source record applies a qualitative comparison to ${p.title.toLowerCase()}. The evidence set combines the cited professional, security, and logging authorities with representative legal-support records. The unit of analysis is the supplied source, the permitted administrative action, the recorded exception, and the authorized reviewer disposition. The comparison separates facts stated by the sources from operational analysis for a supervised offshore legal-support role. It tests whether a reviewer can reconstruct what was present, what was compared, what changed, and what remained unresolved. This is bounded process research, not a survey, compliance certification, legal opinion, market statistic, performance claim, or prediction of any firm’s result. The method intentionally preserves an unknown state when the record does not establish identity, urgency, legal effect, entitlement, meaning, privilege, or outcome.`},
  {heading:'Research limitations', body:`The evidence for ${p.title.toLowerCase()} is limited to public guidance and a hypothetical review sample. Those sources do not decide a matter-specific legal question, engagement term, jurisdiction, client instruction, retention obligation, or professional disposition. A complete administrative record can still contain an inaccurate, stale, incomplete, or misleading source; system fields and timestamps can also differ. The sample cannot prove accuracy across firms, languages, matters, or work periods, and it cannot establish savings, speed, quality rates, or legal outcomes. The firm must define approved fields, access, retention, sampling, stop rules, and the owner who reviews exceptions. Offshore support does not transfer professional responsibility. When evidence conflicts, the support role should preserve the conflict and route it rather than resolve it by assumption.`},
  {heading:'Research conclusion', body:`The evidence supports ${p.title.toLowerCase()} only as a bounded preparation and evidence-preservation lane. A supervised worker may record supplied facts, compare explicit criteria, retain source context, identify uncertainty, and route a neutral handoff. The worker should not turn an administrative signal into a conclusion about legal effect, identity, privilege, conflict, deadline, access, translation, redaction, advice, filing, or client outcome. For LegalServicesOffshore.com, the useful standard is reconstructability: an authorized reviewer can see the source, the action, the non-action, the unresolved question, and the disposition owner. If that chain is missing, the task is outside routine support or its evidence design needs revision. An explicit unresolved state is a valid research finding, not a failed completion.`}
]}));
